## RELION 5.0.1 — Mixed NVIDIA GPU Installation Protocol

# 1. Goal

Build a single RELION 5.0.1 binary that contains native CUDA SASS for two different NVIDIA architectures.

Example:

GPU 1: RTX 4090
Compute capability: 8.9
SASS: sm_89

GPU 2: RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell
Compute capability: 12.0
SASS: sm_120

The final binary should contain:

sm_89.cubin
sm_120.cubin

and no PTX is required for either GPU.

The existing RELION installation should remain untouched.

2. Verify the GPUs and NVIDIA driver

First:

nvidia-smi

Confirm both GPUs are visible.

For example:

RTX 4090
RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell

Then:

nvidia-smi --query-gpu=name,compute_cap,driver_version --format=csv

If compute_cap is not available on your driver, use NVIDIA's published compute-capability information or 
verify with CUDA tools.

Also check:

nvidia-smi
nvcc --version

For the system used in this build:

NVIDIA driver: 595.104.02
CUDA toolkit: 13.3.73

The important distinction is that nvidia-smi reports the driver's supported CUDA API level, whereas nvcc --version reports the toolkit actually used to compile RELION.

3. Make sure the CUDA toolkit is the desired one

In our build, CUDA 13.3 was explicitly selected:

export CUDA_HOME=/usr/local/cuda-13.3
export PATH=$CUDA_HOME/bin:$PATH
export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=$CUDA_HOME/lib64:${LD_LIBRARY_PATH}

Verify:

which nvcc
nvcc --version

Expected:

/usr/local/cuda-13.3/bin/nvcc

and:

Cuda compilation tools, release 13.3, V13.3.73

RELION 5.0.1 supports CUDA 11–13, although CUDA 13 no longer supports Pascal GPUs.

4. Do NOT activate the RELION conda environment for compilation

This is important.

RELION's own documentation recommends not activating the RELION environment during compilation, because conda-provided compilers/libraries/MPI can interfere with the system toolchain.

For my setup, the existing environment has:

relion-5.0

and I want to create a separate environment:

relion-5.0-mixed

The build itself will be performed from the normal shell/base environment.

Check:

echo $CONDA_DEFAULT_ENV

Do not intentionally activate the environment.

5. Obtain RELION 5.0.1 and Create a separate mixed-GPU environment

Use the RELION 5.0.1 source corresponding to:

commit d476e6f6

For example:

cd /mnt/disk/relion_install

git clone https://github.com/3dem/relion.git relion_mixedGPU
cd relion_mixedGPU/relion

git checkout d476e6f6

edit the top line of .yaml to replace "relion-5.0" to "relion-5.0-mixed"

conda env create -f environment_blackwell.yml
conda env update -f environment_blackwell.yml

Then verify:

mamba env list

You should have something like:

relion-5.0
relion-5.0-mixed

The original environment remains untouched.

Verify:

git rev-parse --short HEAD

Expected:

d476e6f6

RELION 5.0.1 is the maintenance release corresponding to d476e6f

The environment should contain everything needed, verify Python:

/home/where-ever-the-miniforge-is/miniforge3/envs/relion-5.0-mixed/bin/python --version

The important point for this build is that CMake explicitly knows which Python executable to use.

8. Prepare a fresh build directory

Do not build over the existing working RELION installation.

My layout was:

/mnt/disk##/relion_install/
    relion_89/
    relion_120/
    relion_mixedGPU/
        relion/
        relion_mixedGPU_src/

Get inside the source tree:

cd /mnt/disk##/relion_install/relion_mixedGPU/relion

rm -rf build
mkdir build
cd build
9. Important RELION CMake modification

This is the critical part.

RELION normally constructs one architecture flag from:

-DCUDA_ARCH=89

which results in something equivalent to:

-arch=sm_89

That is insufficient when one binary must contain multiple native architectures.

The relevant section of CMakeLists.txt originally contains:

set(CUDA_ARCH "" CACHE STRING "CUDA architecture to use")
if (CUDA_ARCH STREQUAL "")
    if (${CUDA_VERSION} VERSION_LESS "13.0")
        message(STATUS "Setting fallback CUDA_ARCH=50 (Pascal)")
        set(CUDARCH "-arch=sm_50")
    else()
        message(STATUS "Setting fallback CUDA_ARCH=75 (Turing)")
        set(CUDARCH "-arch=sm_75")
    endif()
else ()
    message(STATUS "Using provided CUDA_ARCH=${CUDA_ARCH}")
    set(CUDARCH "-arch=sm_${CUDA_ARCH}")
endif ()

Change the three set(CUDARCH...) statements so that CUDARCH is a CMake cache variable:

set(CUDARCH "-arch=sm_50" CACHE STRING "CUDA arch compiler flag override")
set(CUDARCH "-arch=sm_75" CACHE STRING "CUDA arch compiler flag override")
set(CUDARCH "-arch=sm_${CUDA_ARCH}" CACHE STRING "CUDA arch compiler flag override")

This allows us to override CUDARCH from the CMake command line.

10. Modify BuildTypes.cmake for CUDA 13.3

The CUDA 13.3 CCCL headers caused this compilation error:

error: unable to find numeric literal operator ‘operator""Q’
note: use ‘-fext-numeric-literals’ to enable more built-in suffixes

The important detail is that:

-fext-numeric-literals

is a host compiler flag, not a direct NVCC option.

Therefore the correct NVCC syntax is:

-Xcompiler=-fext-numeric-literals

In:

cmake/BuildTypes.cmake

we used:

set(EXTRA_NVCC_FLAGS "-D__INTEL_COMPILER --default-stream per-thread --std=c++17 -Xcompiler=-fext-numeric-literals")

Then:

set(RELION_NVCC_FLAGS "${CUDARCH} ${WARN_DBL} ${EXTRA_NVCC_FLAGS}" CACHE STRING "" FORCE)

The relevant resulting section is:

if(CUDA)
    set(EXTRA_NVCC_FLAGS "-D__INTEL_COMPILER --default-stream per-thread --std=c++17 -Xcompiler=-fext-numeric-literals")
    set(RELION_NVCC_FLAGS "${CUDARCH} ${WARN_DBL} ${EXTRA_NVCC_FLAGS}" CACHE STRING "" FORCE)
endif()

11. Configure CMake with BOTH GPU architectures

For:

RTX 4090      = sm_89
RTX PRO 6000  = sm_120

we used:

cmake \
  -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/mnt/scratch/relion_install/relion_mixedGPU/relion_mixedGPU_src \
  -DDoublePrec_CPU=OFF \
  -DCUDA=ON \
  -DCUDA_ARCH=89 \
  -DCUDARCH="-gencode=arch=compute_89,code=sm_89 -gencode=arch=compute_120,code=sm_120" \
  -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
  -DPYTHON_EXE_PATH=~/miniforge3/envs/relion-5.0/bin/python \
  -DAMDFFTW=ON \
  -DFORCE_OWN_FFTW=ON \
  -DTORCH_HOME_PATH=/mnt/scratch/relion_install/torch_home \
  ..
Why are there two architecture options?

This is slightly unusual but intentional.

-DCUDA_ARCH=89

satisfies RELION's own CUDA architecture logic.

The actual multi-architecture NVCC compilation is controlled by:

-DCUDARCH="..."

which produces:

-gencode=arch=compute_89,code=sm_89
-gencode=arch=compute_120,code=sm_120

The important part is:

code=sm_89
code=sm_120

rather than merely generating PTX.

12. Verify the CMake cache BEFORE compiling

This step is highly recommended.

Run:

grep -E 'CUDA_ARCH:|CUDARCH:|RELION_NVCC_FLAGS|CUDA_NVCC_FLAGS' CMakeCache.txt

You want the Release flags to contain both:

-gencode=arch=compute_89,code=sm_89
-gencode=arch=compute_120,code=sm_120

and:

-Xcompiler=-fext-numeric-literals

For example:

RELION_NVCC_FLAGS_RELEASE:STRING=...
-gencode=arch=compute_89,code=sm_89
-gencode=arch=compute_120,code=sm_120
...
-Xcompiler=-fext-numeric-literals
...
Important CMake cache issue

This caused trouble during our build.

CMake generated Release-specific variables that could retain an older set of NVCC flags.

Therefore, if you change BuildTypes.cmake after running CMake, don't assume the Release cache automatically updates.

Check:

grep 'RELION_NVCC_FLAGS_RELEASE' CMakeCache.txt
grep 'CUDA_NVCC_FLAGS_RELEASE' CMakeCache.txt

If they do not contain:

-Xcompiler=-fext-numeric-literals

and both architectures, correct the cache or, preferably, reconfigure from a clean build directory.

For a reproducible build, the cleanest approach is:

rm -rf build
mkdir build
cd build

then rerun CMake.

13. Build RELION

Start conservatively:

make -j1 VERBOSE=1 2>&1 | tee build.log

Once you know the configuration works, you can increase parallelism:

make -jN

where N is appropriate for your CPU/RAM.

The RELION documentation uses the standard CMake → make workflow and recommends checking the CMake output carefully before compilation.

14. Verify that NVCC is actually compiling BOTH architectures

Search the build log:

grep -o -- '-gencode=arch=compute_[0-9]*,code=sm_[0-9]*' build.log | sort -u

You should see:

-gencode=arch=compute_89,code=sm_89
-gencode=arch=compute_120,code=sm_120

Also:

grep -o -- '-Xcompiler=-fext-numeric-literals' build.log | sort -u

should return:

-Xcompiler=-fext-numeric-literals

15. Build-time SASS verification

Find the CUDA objects:

find . -name '*.cu.o'

Then inspect one:

cuobjdump --list-elf \
./src/apps/CMakeFiles/relion_gpu_util.dir/__/acc/cuda/relion_gpu_util_generated_cuda_helper_functions.cu.o

You should see:

ELF file 1: ...sm_89.cubin
ELF file 2: ...sm_120.cubin

For a comprehensive check, inspect every object in:

lib/librelion_gpu_util.a

For example:

cd lib

for obj in $(ar t librelion_gpu_util.a); do
    ar p librelion_gpu_util.a "$obj" > /tmp/relion_test.o
    echo "===== $obj ====="
    cuobjdump --list-elf /tmp/relion_test.o 2>/dev/null |
        grep -E 'sm_89|sm_120'
done

Every CUDA object should contain both architectures.

16. Install into a separate prefix

Once the build completes:

make install

The separate installation was:

/mnt/scratch/relion_install/relion_mixedGPU/relion_mixedGPU_src

This is important because your existing installations remain untouched.

RELION's documentation also recommends using a separate CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX when installing to a specific location.

17. Verify the installed version

Run:

/mnt/scratch/relion_install/relion_mixedGPU/relion_mixedGPU_src/bin/relion --version

Expected:

RELION version: 5.0.1-commit-d476e6
Precision: BASE=double, CUDA-ACC=single

18. Verify the INSTALLED GPU executable

Do not rely on the GUI relion executable for CUDA verification.

Instead inspect:

cuobjdump --list-elf \
/mnt/scratch/relion_install/relion_mixedGPU/relion_mixedGPU_src/bin/relion_refine

For our build, this returned multiple entries containing:

sm_89.cubin
sm_120.cubin

That is the most important verification.

You can simplify the check:

cuobjdump --list-elf \
/mnt/scratch/relion_install/relion_mixedGPU/relion_mixedGPU_src/bin/relion_refine |
grep -o 'sm_[0-9]*' |
sort -u

Expected:

sm_120
sm_89

19. Verify that the executable contains a CUDA fatbin

Run:

readelf -S \
/mnt/scratch/relion_install/relion_mixedGPU/relion_mixedGPU_src/bin/relion_refine |
grep -Ei 'nv_fatbin|cuda|fatbin'

Expected:

.nv_fatbin
.nvFatBinSegment

This confirms the CUDA device code is embedded in the executable.

20. Verify there is no PTX requirement

Run:

cuobjdump --list-ptx \
/mnt/scratch/relion_install/relion_mixedGPU/relion_mixedGPU_src/bin/relion_refine

For the build we completed, the relevant result was empty.

That means the binary has:

native SASS:
    sm_89
    sm_120

PTX:
    none required

This is different from using:

code=compute_89

which would retain PTX for JIT compilation.

21. Create a launcher for the mixed binary

Keep your existing relion89 and relion120 setup untouched.

Add:

export RELION_MIXED_ROOT="/mnt/scratch/relion_install/relion_mixedGPU/relion_mixedGPU_src"

relion_mixed () {
    local prefix="$RELION_MIXED_ROOT"

    PATH=$(printf '%s' "$PATH" |
        tr ':' '\n' |
        grep -v "^${RELION_ROOT}/relion_" |
        paste -sd:)

    export PATH="$prefix/bin:$PATH"

    export CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES="$GPU_4090,$GPU_PRO6000"

    echo "relion : $(command -v relion)"
    echo "refine : $prefix/bin/relion_refine"

    echo "sass   : $(cuobjdump --list-elf "$prefix/bin/relion_refine" 2>/dev/null |
                    grep -o 'sm_[0-9]*' |
                    sort -u |
                    paste -sd,)"

    echo "ptx    : $(cuobjdump --list-ptx "$prefix/bin/relion_refine" 2>/dev/null |
                    grep -o 'compute_[0-9]*' |
                    sort -u |
                    paste -sd,)"

    echo "gpus   : $CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES"
}

Then:

source ~/.bashrc

Run:

relion_mixed

You should see something similar to:

relion : /mnt/scratch/relion_install/relion_mixedGPU/relion_mixedGPU_src/bin/relion
refine : /mnt/scratch/relion_install/relion_mixedGPU/relion_mixedGPU_src/bin/relion_refine
sass   : sm_120,sm_89
ptx    :
gpus   : GPU-4090-UUID,GPU-PRO6000-UUID

22. Test with an actual RELION GPU job

Launch a small 2D classification test.

While it is running:

watch -n 1 nvidia-smi

or:

nvidia-smi

The RELION process should appear on both GPUs.

In your successful test, you obtained approximately:

RTX 4090:
    22,578 MiB
    66% GPU utilization

RTX PRO 6000:
    96,360 MiB
    68% GPU utilization

with the same:

relion_refine

PID on both GPUs.

That is the final proof that the mixed binary is not merely carrying both architectures—it is actually executing on both GPUs simultaneously.

The key concept

The entire procedure boils down to this:

Normal RELION configuration

-DCUDA_ARCH=89
        ↓
-arch=sm_89
        ↓
binary optimized for one architecture

Our mixed-GPU configuration

-DCUDA_ARCH=89
        +
-DCUDARCH="
    -gencode=arch=compute_89,code=sm_89
    -gencode=arch=compute_120,code=sm_120
"
        ↓
single CUDA fatbin
        ↓
┌──────────────────────────┐
│ native sm_89 SASS        │
│ native sm_120 SASS       │
└──────────────────────────┘
        ↓
one relion_refine binary
        ↓
RTX 4090 + RTX PRO 6000

The CUDARCH override is the central trick. The -Xcompiler=-fext-numeric-literals addition is specifically relevant to the CUDA 13.3/CCCL compilation issue we encountered; it should not be blindly added to unrelated CUDA versions if they don't need it.

Also, RELION explicitly warns that CMake caches configuration, so when changing architecture/compiler flags, a clean build directory is the safest reproducible procedure.
